1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,350
In 2013 a copper mine in Utah knew when its slope would collapse.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,850
It cleared the pit in time. No one was hurt.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,350
In 2024 nobody was measuring the hills above Wayanad, India.

4
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:25,850
Hundreds of people died. The slopes had given the same warning.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:37,850
We're Ground Truth. We built TellTale, which reads that warning with a camera a village can afford.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,850
A slope that is about to fail creeps first, and speeds up.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,850
TellTale watches it with an ordinary camera and measures the movement with OpenCV.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,850
It forecasts when the slope will fail, checks itself, and asks a person to decide.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,850
This is a real landslide, filmed by the US Geological Survey on a day it moved.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,850
TellTale coloured the ground that moved blue, measured to a hundredth of a pixel.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,850
Now a slope from quiet to failure, one photo at a time.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,850
Each photo, TellTale measures how far the ground has moved.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,850
The blue block is creeping downhill, a few millimetres at a time.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,350
As it speeds up, the dots fall in a straight line toward zero.

15
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:47,850
Where the line hits zero is the failure. TellTale gives a window, not a guess.

16
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,850
Here: the slope will fail between day 49 and day 51. It failed on day 50.

17
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,850
Nothing reaches the public unless a person presses Approve.

18
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,850
Before waking anyone, an AI agent, Claude on Amazon Bedrock, checks the warning.

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,850
It measures again two different ways. Real movement gives the same answer both times.

20
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,850
Only then does it page the duty officer, once, with these pictures as evidence.

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,850
This is a live run on AWS, started from the dashboard.

22
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,850
Anyone can press the button and watch it happen, in about three minutes.

23
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:49,850
On the real landslide record it also knows when to stay quiet.

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,850
147 quiet days, zero false alarms. Both movement periods the camera could see were flagged.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,850
We replayed it on six real slopes that collapsed.

26
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,850
It warned before every one. It also raised early alarms, so a person always decides.

27
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:21,850
On a public benchmark it measures to five thousandths of a pixel, ahead of OpenCV's standard methods.

28
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,850
Every number in this video is checked by one command in the code.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,850
Photos go to Amazon S3, a queue feeds a Graviton server running COOL, the cloud-optimised OpenCV.

30
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,850
When there's nothing to do, it switches itself off. A watched slope costs cents a day.

31
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,850
Setting up a slope takes one photo: mark the stable ground, and the slope to watch.

32
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,850
Today, one sensor in one borehole costs five thousand euros or more.

33
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,850
TellTale watches a whole slope for about a dollar forty a month.

34
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,850
It can't warn of sudden collapses, and it can't see at night. It says so.

35
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,850
TellTale. The dashboard is live. Try it yourself.
